ply-Side Sustainability (New York: Columbia University Press, 2003). 26. M. C. Roco and W. S. Bainbridge, 'Executive Summary,' in NSF! DOC-sponsored report, Converging Technologies for Improving Human Performance: Nanotechnology, Biotechnology, Information Technology and Cognitive Science (Arlington, Virginia, 2002), available at http://www.wtec. org!ConvergingTechnologies!Report!NBIC_report.pdf, p. xiii. 27. Ibid., p. 1. 28. Geoffrey Vickers, The Undirected Society, Essays on the Human Implications of Industrialization in Canada (Toronto: University of Toronto Press, 1959), p.55.",3,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Substantiation/References,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of norms; treat as noise unless context warrants.,e1a09b05cf,Global governance as international organization,,
stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z,stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z_0000732,False,"Globalization_ environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures.pdf",6,Unspecified,norms,TH002,Norms,Pass 2 (theme + validated synonym dictionary),"Verbatim paragraph excerpt (PDF text layer), extracted at paragraph boundary","d the IMF, establishing a number of formal treaties, international regim systems of global governance, as well as, sometimes, structures of **norms** and ues. Between 1909 and 1989, the total number of these IG ... Os has grown from 3 almost 300 (Held, 1996, p. 346). Most of them have a rather technical charac such as the Universal Postal Union, the World Meteorological Association, an host of other bodies, but others hav","32 ORGANIZATION & ENVIRONMENT / March 2006 mal institutional structure are more precise than Tarrow's distinction betw strong and weak states, whereas the triads of facilitation, assimilation, and coo ation and of repression, confrontation, and polarization could be seen as indicat of Tarrow's two criteria of facilitative and repressive elites. Based on this elaboration of POS, a number of hypotheses have been develo about the relationships between different impacts, action repertoires, level mobilization, and organizational structures of new social movements on one ha and POSs on the other hand (Kriesi et al., 1995; van der Heijden, 1997). F instance, when comparing modern-day Germany and the Netherlands, Kriesi et concluded that Germany's open political input structure (""weak state"") and ex sive elite strategies was most favourable for having ""reactive impacts.""3 Proced impacts were not very likely because of the prevailing exclusive strategy of p cal authorities toward challengers (Kriesi et al., 1995, p. 213). In the Netherlan on the other hand, procedural impacts became more likely because the authori tended to adopt inclusive strategies when facing challengers. In addition, the tively strong Dutch state, combined with a prevailing inclusive elite strategy, ated favorable conditions for proactive impacts, because the government was at t same time willing and able to respond positively to the demands of new soc movements (Kriesi et al., 1995, pp. 213-214). With respect to the relationship between POS and action repertoires, van d Heijden suggested that a small number of points of access for new social mo ments (little territorial decentralization, large horizontal concentration of sta power, etc.) enhanced the occurrence of unconventional actions, simply beca other possibilities were hardly available. A large number of points of ac thwarted the occurrence of unconventional actions (van der Heijden, 1997, p. 3 Operationalizing International Political Opportunity Structure How, based on the different variants of POS described above, could the conc of IPOS be operationalized? Three preliminary remarks should be made. First of all, whereas national POS always refers to a state, obviously there is such thing as an international or global state. IPOS is a composite of a number International Governmental Organizations (IGOs) like the UN, the EU, the Wo Bank, and the IMF, establishing a number of formal treaties, international regim systems of global governance, as well as, sometimes, structures of **norms** and ues. Between 1909 and 1989, the total number of these IGOs has grown from 3 almost 300 (Held, 1996, p. 346). Most of them have a rather technical charac such as the Universal Postal Union, the World Meteorological Association, an host of other bodies, but others have a highly political character. Of course, in m cases, this last category represents the most important part of IPOS for soc movements. Second, due to processes of globalization, the level of political decision ma to a considerable extent has shifted from the national to the international level national (and subnational) levels, however, remain important for co-decision implementation of politics (""multilevel governance""). This implies that env mental movements will also act at these different levels of politics (""mult environmentalism""). Third, IPOS may be partly different for different social movements. Whe for instance, the WTO is of vital importance for environmental movemen because of environmental trade restrictions, it might be less important for ments such as the human and gay rights movements. This content downloaded from ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀102.64.114.122 on Tue, 26 Nov 2024 19:47:43 UTC␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/terms",5,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Definition/Identification,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of norms; treat as noise unless context warrants.,eb2433c731,"Globalization environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures",,10.1177/1086026605285452
stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z,stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z_0000733,False,"Globalization_ environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures.pdf",7,Unspecified,norms,TH002,Norms,Pass 2 (theme + validated synonym dictionary),"Verbatim paragraph excerpt (PDF text layer), extracted at paragraph boundary",". 13), such as the Biodiversity and Climate Conven tions. For various international regimes, the UN also controls the implementation of the **norms** and rules agreed upon. What are the opportunities offered by ... the UN to environmental movements and EMOs? As for the first variable, formal institutional structure, the UN political structure offers challengers formal access. Since its very beginning, the UN has been for","van der Heijden / ENVIRONMENTAL MOVEMENTS 33 What would IPOS look like? In this article, four different dimensions of IPOS are distinguished, largely covering or summarizing the different dimensions of national POS as analysed above: 1. formal institutional structure (open-closed) 2. informal elite strategies (integrative-exclusive) 3. configuration of power (divided elite-united elite) 4. political output structure (weak-strong) Following Tarrow, all four dimensions will be seen as four separate continua rather than as reflecting merely polar opposites, characteristic of different types of IGOs. Consequently, statements on characteristics of IGOs will be relational ones, phrased in forms such as, ""the political output structure of IGO A will be weaker than that of IGO B,"" and so on. INTERNATIONAL POLITICAL OPPORTUNITY STRUCTURE FOR TRANSNATIONAL ENVIRONMENTAL MOVEMENTS From the point of view of transnational environmental movements, the 300 IGOs as distinguished by Held could be subdivided into several differ kinds: global IGOs with multiple goals, among which environmental prote one (best example: the UN); regional IGOs with multiple goals, among whic ronmental protection is one (best example: the EU); and global IGOs with s goals that have consequences for the environment, either directly, like the Bank, or more indirectly, like the WTO. In this section, the UN, EU, Worl and the WTO will represent the different elements of IPOS. Other IGOs th have been included are the IMF (Korten, 1998), the Asian Development B NAFTA (Grossman, 2000; Marchak, 1998), the International Tropical T Organization (ITTO), and several others. The United Nations Despite its well-known limitations, the UN is the most important global politi cal power centre, where decisions, many of them involving every country and every citizen in the world, are made. The UN is the platform for building multilat eral environmental agreements (Mason, 2004, p. 78) and international political regimes (Porter et al., 2000, p. 13), such as the Biodiversity and Climate Conven tions. For various international regimes, the UN also controls the implementation of the **norms** and rules agreed upon. What are the opportunities offered by the UN to environmental movements and EMOs? As for the first variable, formal institutional structure, the UN political structure offers challengers formal access. Since its very beginning, the UN has been for mally open to social movement organizations, article 71 of the UN Charter explain ing the legal basis for such access (Passy, 1999, p. 153). These organizations have, however, only consultative status, which allows them to make oral and present written statements—in commissions, subcommissions, conferences, and working groups—but does not give them the right to vote, that is, the right to participate in political decisions (p. 154). As for the second variable, informal elite strategies, Passy (1999) has observed that to the UN social movements are important pools of knowledge. For this rea This content downloaded from ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀102.64.114.122 on Tue, 26 Nov 2024 19:47:43 UTC␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/terms",8,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Substantiation/References,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of norms; treat as noise unless context warrants.,eb2433c731,"Globalization environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures",,10.1177/1086026605285452
