osts of compliance, inability to monitor compliance, and finally, lo administrative capacity (Porter et al., 2000, pp. 150-153). In conclusion, UN IPOS for environmental movements could be summarized follows: Formal institutional structure: (rather) open Informal elite strategies: (very) integrative Configuration of power: (very) divided elite Output structure: (very) weak The European Union Although in academic literature the EU has been framed in different way (intergovernmentalism, federalism, etc.), the past decade has witnessed the ""g ernance turn"" in European studies (Jordan, 2001). Seen from this perspective, EU has evolved into a system of multilevel governance, as opposed to state-l government. Environmental politics is a case in point. With more than 300 items environmental legislation now passed at the EU level, the past three decades ref the development from near nonexistent European environmental politics to t present situation in which most environmental legislation is decided on in Brusse but implemented, contested, and reshaped at the national and subnational le (e.g., Sbragia, 2000; Zito, 2000). What does the European POS for environmental movements and EMOs loo like? With its dispersed competencies, contending but interlocked institutions, shifting agendas, EU multilevel governance opens multiple points of access interests (Hooghe & Marks, 2001, p. 28). Four key European policy-making i tutions are identified below: the Commission, the Council of Ministers, the Co of Justice, and the Parliament (Wallace & Wallace, 2000). The European Commission is a hybrid executive/civil service, formally resp sible for initiating and drafting legislation, which it submits to the Council of M isters and the Parliament for approval. The Commission structure is open, but it a bias toward information that will enable it to formulate legislation that can sus majorities in the Council and Parliament (Marks & McAdam, 1999, p. 105). So groups, including EMOs, that are part of the system of interest representation facilitated by the Commission (indicative of integrative elite strategies; Webs This content downloaded from ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀102.64.114.122 on Tue, 26 Nov 2024 19:47:43 UTC␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/terms",7,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Explanation/Elaboration,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of rule of law; treat as noise unless context warrants.,eb2433c731,"Globalization environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures",,10.1177/1086026605285452
