mber of EMOs that will try to influence its poli tics by conventional means and into largely procedural and reactive impacts. The political influence of transnational EMOs on the establishment of two major global environmental regimes, the climate and biodiversity conventions, has been charted by Arts (1998). Whereas the participation of EMOs in the formation phase of the Biodiversity Convention was restricted to groups like WWF and IUCN, their participation numbers grew immensely during the implementation phase (1992-1997), when 75 to 100 NGOs attended the meetings of the Interna tional Negotiation Committee (INC). Unconventional actions, however, did not take place (Arts, 1998, pp. 169-170). As for assessing the impact of EMOs, Arts subdivided the convention into dif ferent parts, each dealing with a different topic: indigenous people, in-situ conser vation, marine biodiversity, financial mechanisms, and so on. In sum, EMOs exerted only substantial influence on the inclusion of the notion of intrinsic value and of the paragraph on women in the Preamble of the Convention. They influ enced some aspects of, for instance, in-situ conservation measures (Arts, 1998, p. 227), but generally speaking, the impact of EMOs was rather limited indeed. With respect to the Framework Convention on Climate Change (FCCC), EMOs were able to participate in the negotiations as observers, both in the sessions of the INC and the meetings of the Conference of Parties (COPs). INCs were visited by approximately 50 to 100 NGOs, one third of which were environmental organiza tions. Most of these EMOs collaborated under the umbrella of the (global) Climate Action Network and its seven regional offices (Arts, 1998, p. 109). As for their impact, EMOs had no direct impact on the FCCC text. With regard to the period after its adoption (1992-1995), EMOs substantially impacted the AOSIS (Alliance of Small Island States) Protocol, whereas they had some impact on the Joint Implementation Pilot Phase, and the Berlin Mandate (Arts, 1998, pp. 155-156).3 These findings largely corroborate this article's preliminary propositions with regard to the number of NGO participants, the existence of unconventional actions, and the type of impacts. It is interesting to note that most NGO influence was achieved during the implementation of the convention, rather than during its for mation. The emergence of the Berlin Mandate was also one of the very few instances during which (complementary) protest actions took place (Arts, 1998, p. 236). This content downloaded from ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀102.64.114.122 on Tue, 26 Nov 2024 19:47:43 UTC␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/terms",9,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Explanation/Elaboration,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of compliance; treat as noise unless context warrants.,eb2433c731,"Globalization environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures",,10.1177/1086026605285452
stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z,stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z_0000738,False,"Globalization_ environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures.pdf",14,Introduction,participation,TH007,Participation,Pass 2 (theme + validated synonym dictionary),"Verbatim paragraph excerpt (PDF text layer), extracted at paragraph boundary","uropean Union Like the UN, the EU has an open institutional structure and the elite strat are relatively integrative. As a consequence, the **participation** of a large numb EMOs, maintaining a conventional act ... ion repertoire, and the occurrence of dominantly procedural and reactive impacts could be expected, although less at the UN level. The increasing role of the EU with respect to environmental policy makin reflec","40 ORGANIZATION & ENVIRONMENT / March 2006 The European Union Like the UN, the EU has an open institutional structure and the elite strat are relatively integrative. As a consequence, the **participation** of a large numb EMOs, maintaining a conventional action repertoire, and the occurrence of dominantly procedural and reactive impacts could be expected, although less at the UN level. The increasing role of the EU with respect to environmental policy makin reflected in the emergence of a number of environmental organizations in Brus The first one was the European Environmental Bureau (EEB), founded in 19 European federation of environmental groups. During the 1980s and 1990s, E pean branches of Friends of the Earth, Greenpeace, WWF, Birdlife Internati and the Climate Action Network were founded, as well as the European Feder for Transport and Environment (T&E). In addition, in recent years, about a other European environmental groups were founded (Rucht, 2001; Web 1998). Militant protest tactics for environmental protection during this period scattered, rare, and small, although some transnational environmental protest occur in Brussels (and Strasbourg), organized by Greenpeace, Friends of the E and animal rights groups (Rucht, 2001, p. 128). Basically, however, the action ertoire of the groups was limited to negotiations with officials, lobbying, e studies, and press releases. As for the impact of environmental organizations, in the literature no proact impacts are mentioned. One example, among many, of a reactive impact cou the outcome of the European conflict over the labeling of genetically mod products (Kettnaker, 2001). An important example of a procedural impact could be the introduction of tegic Environmental Assessments (SEAs) for Trans-European transport Netw (TENs), an extensive network of cross-border high-speed trains, motorways, waterways, aimed at providing the infrastructure for a single European mar Although the literature does not include clear examples of proactive impac EMOs, one proactive impact of European environmental policy making can identified. European environmental legislation has pressed many ""laggard"" m ber states to adopt more stringent environmental policies than these state before EU action (Sbragia, 2000; Zito, 2000). In this respect, the EU as an env mental policy maker could be conceived as an ally rather than as an antagon environmental movements in many European member states. The World Bank The formal institutional structure of the World Bank is closed and its elite st gies are not very integrative; as a consequence, unconventional actions cou expected. The political output structure is strong and its elite are united; as a quence, no proactive impacts for environmental movements are to be expect Until the early 1980s, the environmental politics of the World Bank had a profile for NGOs. The first pressure for change occurred with the Polonoroe 1,500-kilometre highway from Brazil's densely populated south-central re into the sparsely populated northwest Amazon. The bank's environmental cr held up Polonoroeste as the bank's biggest and most disastrous involvement i est colonization in the tropics (Keck & Sikkink, 1998, ch. 4; Khagram, 2000, Wade, 2000, pp. 75-76). This content downloaded from ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀102.64.114.122 on Tue, 26 Nov 2024 19:47:43 UTC␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/terms",4,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Substantiation/References,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of participation; treat as noise unless context warrants.,eb2433c731,"Globalization environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures",,10.1177/1086026605285452
stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z,stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z_0000739,False,"Globalization_ environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures.pdf",16,Conclusion,participation,TH007,Participation,Pass 2 (theme + validated synonym dictionary),"Verbatim paragraph excerpt (PDF text layer), extracted at paragraph boundary","ires, and impacts of transnational mental movements and EMOs. IPOS, for instance, may help to explain wh like the UN and the EU provoke the **participation** of a large number of trans environmental lobby group ... s. It also seems to predict that the proactive im these groups will remain limited. IPOS also helps to explain why closed tively exclusive organizations like the World Bank and the WTO provok unconventional act","42 ORGANIZATION & ENVIRONMENT / March 2006 ment mechanism. NGOs themselves have argued strongly that membership o CTE should be enlarged to include NGOs, but currently NGOs are not allowe participate (Boas & Vevatne, 2004, pp. 101-102). Despite these institutional ers, some moderate EMOs, mainly from the United States, have had some ence on the handling of environmental disputes, for instance, the shrimp-t dispute (Desombre & Barkin, 2002). On the other hand, the People's Global Action and other parts of the an globalization movement presented a radical departure from attempts to lobby WTO and influence debates (Smith, 2002; Wissenburg, 2004). In Seattle, the i of trade liberalization brought together a coalition, united against an organiz that appeared to put the profit and good of multinational corporations befor interests of people and the environment (Hoad, 2000, p. 125). This rejection approach perceives environmental management as a technocratic problem-so approach that is not concerned with the eradication of environmental degrad as mediated through social, economic, and political relations, but with the sm functioning of the system (Williams & Ford, 1999, p. 287). Since the Seattle ing, developing countries in particular have forced lobbying alliances with n ern development NGOs over issues such as opposition to agricultural subsidie the United States and the EU (Mason, 2004, p. 571). As for this article's propositions, the WTO has provoked many unconvent actions, Seattle being a paradigm case. Most of the time, WTO elites seem to ate rather like-minded, and as a consequence, the impacts of EMOs are proce (e.g., symposia on trade and environment) rather than proactive. In some however, EMOs and even the environmental part of the antiglobalization m ment appear to be able to drive a wedge between WTO elites, and in such c very important proactive impacts are not impossible. CONCLUSION The main question to be answered in this article was to what extent it is p to turn the concept of POS from the national to the international level in explain or even predict the different successes, strategies, and action reperto environmental movements at that level. To contribute to theory building make suggestions for further empirical research, in this article the concept was developed. Second, this article developed and provided initial evidence propositions resulting from this concept by means of a concise secondary of literature on four IGOs. Like national POS, the concept of IPOS seems to be a useful tool for an the different strategies, action repertoires, and impacts of transnational mental movements and EMOs. IPOS, for instance, may help to explain wh like the UN and the EU provoke the **participation** of a large number of trans environmental lobby groups. It also seems to predict that the proactive im these groups will remain limited. IPOS also helps to explain why closed tively exclusive organizations like the World Bank and the WTO provok unconventional actions by groups with a rejectionist stance, like the Pe Global Action. It also tentatively predicts that the number of unconven actions will decline if and when the World Bank and the WTO adop integrative elite strategies. Transnational environmental campaigns, however, always take place at ent levels (global, regional, national: multilevel environmentalism) and th not only alter the impacts as predicted by IPOS theory but also, sometimes This content downloaded from ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀102.64.114.122 on Tue, 26 Nov 2024 19:47:43 UTC␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/terms",3,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Explanation/Elaboration,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of participation; treat as noise unless context warrants.,eb2433c731,"Globalization environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures",,10.1177/1086026605285452
stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z,stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z_0000740,False,"Globalization_ environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures.pdf",17,Unspecified,participation,TH007,Participation,Pass 2 (theme + validated synonym dictionary),"Verbatim paragraph excerpt (PDF text layer), extracted at paragraph boundary","oliti authorities. Procedural impacts, finally, refer to access to the system by challengers, f instance, by formal recognition or by their **participation** in consultation procedures (Kri et al., 1995, p. 210 ... ). 4. Apart from these, the World Bank Group also includes the International Finance Co poration, the Multilateral Investment Guarantee Agency, and the International Centre Settlement of Investment Disputes. Al","van der Heijden / ENVIRONMENTAL MOVEMENTS 43 the very nature of a set of political opportunities. For instance, as American EMOs successfully lobbied the American government with respect to World Bank lendings for the construction of large dams, this led to splits in the World Bank elite and, as a consequence, to the canceling of several dam projects. The multilevel character of transnational environmental campaigns helps to explain how closed input structures and strong output structures could lead to favourable proactive impacts when EMOs and movements succeed in driving a wedge between political elites. Likewise, European environmental legislation could press laggard states to adopt a more stringent environmental policy, advocated and monitored by EMOs. As Tarrow has emphasized, POS also has a dynamic aspect, and transnational environmental campaigns take place in different stages with different cleavages within and among elites. Whereas, for instance, EMOs could hardly impact the for mation of the climate convention, they could influence the implementation of the convention (AOSIS Protocol, Joint Implementation, Berlin Mandate). It is proba bly no coincidence that during this stage, some complementary unconventional protest actions took place. In this article, a distinction was made between seven different parts of the envi ronmental movement. Obviously, the roles these different parts play in interna tional environmental politics are very diverse, ranging from replacing government in the implementation or monitoring of environmental politics to rejecting the very structures of the World Bank and of the WTO by some parts of the antiglobal ization movement. More specific research, especially case studies, is necessary to chart the relationship between IPOS and the different parts of the environmental movement and to deduce strategic recommendations to further improve their impacts. NOTES 1. In most literature, the terms (environmental) nongovernmental organization (NGO and social movement organization (SMO) are used almost interchangeably. Although the are important differences, many of these groups have their origins in the environmen movement, and for that reason, in this article, systematically the term environmental mov ment organization (EMO) will be used, referring to both environmental NGOs and SMOs. 2. In this article, the term transnational environmental movement will be used in opp tion to international environmental movement to emphasize that the (universal) interests the movement transcend the (national) interests of individual states but that movements ha to operate within the framework of interstate cooperation. 3. Reactive impacts imply the prevention of new disadvantages, that is, challengers avoid a worsening of the situation with regard to their goals. Proactive impacts imply the introdu tion of new advantages, that is, challengers obtain substantive concessions by politi authorities. Procedural impacts, finally, refer to access to the system by challengers, f instance, by formal recognition or by their **participation** in consultation procedures (Kri et al., 1995, p. 210). 4. Apart from these, the World Bank Group also includes the International Finance Co poration, the Multilateral Investment Guarantee Agency, and the International Centre Settlement of Investment Disputes. All five specialize in different aspects of development. 5. The AOSIS Protocol was aimed at strengthening the FCCC to better protect some o the southern victim states against the consequences of climate change. Joint implementation refers to the possibility for (industrialized) countries to realize some of their emission redu tion obligations in other countries by means of technological assistance to those countrie The Berlin Mandate was a mandate, agreed upon during the 1995 Berlin Conference of P ties, to start negotiations on strengthening the commitments for the period beyond 2 through the adoption of a protocol or another legal instrument (Arts, 1998). This content downloaded from ␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀102.64.114.122 on Tue, 26 Nov 2024 19:47:43 UTC␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀ All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/terms",4,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Definition/Identification,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of participation; treat as noise unless context warrants.,eb2433c731,"Globalization environmental movements, and international political opportunity structures",,10.1177/1086026605285452
stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z,stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z_0000741,False,Greening the United Nations charter_World politics in the anthropocene.pdf,2,Unspecified,fragmentation,TH001,Fragmentation,Pass 2 (theme + validated synonym dictionary),"Verbatim paragraph excerpt (PDF text layer), extracted at paragraph boundary",essing major shortcomings in the current system: Lack of integration of economic and environmental policies in the UN system; institutional **fragmentation** and weakness of the environmental pillar of sustaina ... ble development; lack of high-level regulatory competence and oversight regarding areas beyond national jurisdiction; and insufficient integration of scientific insights into political decision-making. The refo,"2 | EARTH System governance working paper No. 21 Citation This paper can be cited as: Biermann, Frank. 2012. Greening the United Nations Charter. World Politics in the Anthropocene. Earth System Governance Working Paper No. 21. Lund and Amsterdam: Earth System Governance Project. All rights remain with the author. Author's Contact: Frank Biermann, Amsterdam Global Change Institute, VU University Amsterdam. De Boelelaan 1087, 1081 HV Amsterdam, The Netherlands. frank.biermann@ivm.vu.nl Working Paper Series Editor Ayşem Mert Amsterdam Global Change Institute VU University Amsterdam (workingpapers@earthsystemgovernance.org) Abstract A constitutional turn is needed to bring the UN system in line with the urgent needs of planetary stewardship and earth system governance in the 21st century. Yet how this could be organized in practice remains a challenge and subject to political and scholarly debate. This paper contributes to this debate by outlining four reforms of the UN system that would advance global decision-making by addressing major shortcomings in the current system: Lack of integration of economic and environmental policies in the UN system; institutional **fragmentation** and weakness of the environmental pillar of sustainable development; lack of high-level regulatory competence and oversight regarding areas beyond national jurisdiction; and insufficient integration of scientific insights into political decision-making. The reforms proposed would together create an Earth Alliance in the UN system, consisting of a high-level UN Sustainable Development Council, a World Environment Organization, a UN Trusteeship Council for Areas beyond National Jurisdiction, and an UN Global Environmental Assessment Commission.",4,False,SUGGEST_REVIEW,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Explanation/Elaboration,Draft memo: relevant discussion tagged as fragmentation; verify alignment with theme definition and adjust tag if the construct boundary is better captured elsewhere.,173fe6145a,Greening the United Nations charter World politics in the anthropocene,,
