d organizations from industrialized countries. This has also been identified as a problem as regards the UN Commission on Sustainable Development; see Kaasa, op cit., at p. 115-116. 37 See for instance P. Pattberg, “Private Governance and the South. Lessons from Global Forest Politics,” Third World Quarterly 27 (2006): 579–593. 38 See G. Schouten and P. Glasbergen, “Creating Legitimacy in Global Private Governance. The Case of the Roundtable on Sustainable Palm Oil,” Ecological Economics 70 (2011): 1891–1899.",4,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Explanation/Elaboration,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of accountability; treat as noise unless context warrants.,173fe6145a,Greening the United Nations charter World politics in the anthropocene,,
