ve relatively limited capacities; the orchestration deficit is real even if it is significantly smaller than the IOG deficit. Given these circumstances, IO orchestration offers a promising addition to the repertoire of IOs that may improve their performance over a large and growing area of international regulation. IOs have a special place in TNG because of the nature of the underlying regulatory issues and the limitations of decentralized RSS. Individual RSS schemes sometimes work at cross-purposes, and satisfactory schemes have not emerged in all the areas where they could be beneficial. Only IOs have the global reach, representativeness and **legitimacy** to orchestrate a successful TNG system. A few IOs—such as UNEP and the UN Secretary-General’s office—have pioneered such approaches, but many others have not yet risen to the challenge. There is enormous potential to broaden and deepen IO engagement. This is the challenge and the opportunity for IOs: to improve their performance through orchestration. Acknowledgements We thank the editors, Tamar Gutner and Alexander Thompson, and the anonymous reviewers for valuable comments on this paper. References Abbott, K. W., & Snidal, D. (1998). Why states act through formal international organizations. Journal of Conflict Resolution, 42(1), 3–32. Abbott, K. W., & Snidal, D. (2000). Hard and Soft Law in International Governance. International Organization, 54(3), 421–456. Abbott, K. W., & Snidal, D. (2005). The international standards process: Setting and applying global business norms. In P. Nobel (Ed.), International standards and the law (pp. 106–142). Berne: Staempfli. Abbott, K. W., & Snidal, D. (2009a). The governance triangle: Regulatory standards, institutions and the shadow of the state. In W. Mattli & N. Woods (Eds.), (pp. 44–88). Abbott, K. W., & Snidal, D. (2009b). Strengthening international regulation through transnational new governance: overcoming the orchestration deficit. Vanderbilt Journal of Transnational Law, 42(2), 501–578. Andonova, L. B. (2010). Public-Private partnerships for the earth: politics and patterns of hybrid authority in the multilateral system. Global Environmental Politics, 10(2), 25–53. International regulation without international government: Improving IO performance... 341",8,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Explanation/Elaboration,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of legitimacy; treat as noise unless context warrants.,cfbb3fc78b,International regulation without international government- Improving IO performance through orchestration Abott&Snidar,,10.1007/s11558-010-9092-3
