ects of the Cartagena Protocol on Biosafety to the biodiversity convention, see Aarti Gupta, “Governing Trade in Genetically Modified Organisms: The Cartagena Protocol on Biosafety,” Environment, vol. 42 (4), (2000): 23–33. 34 Najam 2003. 35 Another issue are the conditions for, and processes through which major reforms of the UN system in general and specific policy sectors can be achieved, e.g. regarding questions of the relevant actors, power distribution and interests. This set of questions is beyond the scope of this chapter, but is being currently analyzed by the MANUS research group (“Managers of Global Change: Effectiveness and Learning of International Environmental Organizations”) within the international Global Governance Project GLOGOV.ORG. See Biermann and Bauer 2004a, 2005; and Biermann and Siebenhüner 2007. 36 Peter Newell, “New Environmental Architectures and the Search for Effectiveness” Global Environmental Politics, vol. 1 (1), (2001): 35–44; and Oberthür and Gehring (2005).",2,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Definition/Identification,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of multilateral environmental agreements; treat as noise unless context warrants.,173edd6860,REFORMING GLOBAL ENVIRONMENTAL GOVERNANCE- FROM UNEP TOWARDS A WORLD ENVIRONMENT ORGANIZATION Biermann,,
stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z,stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z_0001537,False,Relations Between International Organisations_.pdf,1,Introduction,fragmentation,TH001,Fragmentation,Pass 2 (theme + validated synonym dictionary),"Verbatim paragraph excerpt (PDF text layer), extracted at paragraph boundary","re of global efforts to mitigate and adapt to climate change. For this reason, an extensive body of literature on climate regime complexes, **fragmentation**, climate policy integration, institutional interacti ... on and interaction management has emerged in recent years (Biermann et al. 2009 ; Gehring and Faude 2013 ; Oberthür and Pozarowska 2013 ; van Asselt and Zelli 2014 ; Zelli and van Asselt 2013 ). While most of t","649 © The Author(s) 2017 R. Biermann, J.A. Koops (eds.), Palgrave Handbook of Inter-Organizational Relations in World Politics, DOI 10.1057/978-1-137-36039-7_31 1 INTRODUCTION Inter-organisational interaction is of great importance in the area of climate change since it is one of the most complex policy problems of our times. 1 Coordination amongst and confl ict between different organisations are crucial factors for the success and failure of global efforts to mitigate and adapt to climate change. For this reason, an extensive body of literature on climate regime complexes, **fragmentation**, climate policy integration, institutional interaction and interaction management has emerged in recent years (Biermann et al. 2009 ; Gehring and Faude 2013 ; Oberthür and Pozarowska 2013 ; van Asselt and Zelli 2014 ; Zelli and van Asselt 2013 ). While most of these studies focus on institutions, defi ned as relatively stable sets of rules and practices, this chapter applies their main concepts and fi ndings to international organisations, the physical brick-and-mortar entities involved in global climate governance. It shows that an extensive web of interaction amongst various organisations has emerged. The depth and intensity of these inter-organisational relations however are relatively light and superfi cial. The system is loosely integrated, including unilateral climate activities, inter-organisational information exchange but also some closer cooperation in the form of joint programmes. The United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) is the hub of this system. Global climate governance is characterised by a proliferation of private and public–private initiatives in addition to the complex web of international organisations and institutions (Green 2013 ). While some organisations and initiatives include a large number of countries, various so-called minilateral Relations Between International Organisations in Combating Climate Change Katja Biedenkopf CHAPTER 31 K. Biedenkopf ( ) Leuven International and European Studies , KU Leuven , Leuven , Belgium Palgrave Handbook of Inter-Organizational Relations in World Politics, edited by Rafael Biermann, and Joachim A. Koops, Palgrave Macmillan UK, 2016. ProQuest Ebook Central, http://ebookcentral.proquest.com/lib/uow/detail.action?docID=4751360. Created from uow on 2024-11-26 20:15:57. Copyright © 2016. Palgrave Macmillan UK. All rights reserved.",9,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Substantiation/References,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of fragmentation; treat as noise unless context warrants.,7a8a846a0e,Relations Between International Organisations,,10.1057/978-1-137-36039-7_31
