 on the Human Environment Stockholm, June 5–16, 1972, UN Doc.A/CONF.48/14/Rev.1, pp.3–28. 9. ‘Indira Gandhi’s Address at the Stockholm Conference in 1972ʹ, Plenary Session of United Nations Conference on Human Environment Stockholm, June 14, 1972. 10. Report of the United Nations Conference on Environment and Development, Rio de Janeiro, June 3–14, 1992, UN Doc./CONF.151/26 (Vol. IV). 11. Maria Ivanova, ‘UNEP in global environmental governance: design, leadership, location,’ Global Environmental Politics, 10 (1), 2010, p. 31. 12. ‘Institutional and Financial Arrangements for International Environmental Cooperation,’ UNGA Resolution, December 15, 1972, UN Doc. A/RES/2997(XXVII). 13. Ibid. 14. Maria Ivanova, ‘Colouring the UN Environmental: The Catalytic Role of the UN Environment Programme’, Global Governance, 26, 2020, p.317. 15. Convention on the Preservation of Marine Pollution (London 1972), Convention on Long- Range Transboundary Air Pollution (Geneva 1979), Convention Concerning the Protection of the World Cultural and Natural Heritage (Paris 1972), Protocol on Substances that Deplete the Ozone Layer (Montreal 1987) and Convention on the Conservation of Migratory Species of Wild Animals (Bonn 1979), are a few examples. 16. Maurice Strong, Secretary-General of the Conference in his closing remarks described UNCED as ‘the largest high-level intergovernmental conference ever held on our planet.” See no. 10. 17. ‘Report of the World Commission on Environment and Development: Our Common Future’, Official Records of the General Assembly, Forty-second Session, Supplement No, 25 (A/42/ 25). 18. UNGA Resolutions, ‘Protection of global climate for present and future generations of mankind’, December 6 1988, UN Doc. A/RES/43/53; ‘Protection of global climate for present and future generations of mankind’, December 22, 1989, UN Doc. A/RES/44/207; ‘United Nations Conference on Environment and Development,’ December 22, 1989, UN Doc. A/RES/44/228. 19. Rio Declaration on Environment and Development, Principle 1, Doc. A/CONF.151/26/Rev.1, Annexe 1 (August 12, 1992). 20. UNGA Resolution, ‘Protection of global climate for present and future generations of mankind’, December 21, 1990, UN Doc. A/RES/45/212. 21. ‘United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change 1992ʹ at https://unfccc.int/ resource/docs/convkp/conveng.pdf (Accessed on August 3, 2020) 22. The targets for the first commitment period of the Kyoto Protocol cover emissions of the six greenhouse gases, namely: Carbon dioxide (CO2); Methane (CH4); Nitrous oxide (N2O); Hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs); Perfluorocarbons (PFCs); and Sulphur hexafluoride (SF6). See Kyoto Protocol Reference Manual on Accounting of Emissions an Assigned Amount UNFCC 488 Rajeesh Kumar",3,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Substantiation/References,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of international environmental law; treat as noise unless context warrants.,df399f5d19,The United Nations and Global Environmental Governance Rajeesh Kumar2020,,10.1080/09700161.2020.1824462
