stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z,stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z_0002215,False,Transforming governance and institutions for global sustainability- key insights from the_Earth System Governance Project.pdf,1,Introduction,resilience,TH006,Resilience,Pass 2 (theme + validated synonym dictionary),"Verbatim paragraph excerpt (PDF text layer), extracted at paragraph boundary","niversity, United States 7 Durham University, United Kingdom 8 Yale University, United States 9 University of Waterloo, Canada 10 Stockholm **Resilience** Centre, Stockholm University, Sweden 11 Beijer Institu ... te, Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, Sweden 12 Wageningen University and Research Centre, Netherlands 13 UNESCO-IHE Institute for Water Education, Delft, Netherlands 14 University of Massachusetts at Amherst,","Transforming governance and institutions for global sustainability: key insights from the Earth System Governance Project Frank Biermann1,2, Kenneth Abbott3, Steinar Andresen4, Karin Ba¨ ckstrand2, Steven Bernstein5, Michele M Betsill6, Harriet Bulkeley7, Benjamin Cashore8, Jennifer Clapp9, Carl Folke10,11, Aarti Gupta12, Joyeeta Gupta1,13, Peter M Haas14, Andrew Jordan15, Norichika Kanie16,17, Tatiana Kluva´ nkova´ -Oravska´ 18, Louis Lebel19, Diana Liverman20,21, James Meadowcroft22, Ronald B Mitchell23, Peter Newell24, Sebastian Oberthu¨ r25, Lennart Olsson2, Philipp Pattberg1, Roberto Sa´ nchez-Rodrı´guez26,27, Heike Schroeder15, Arild Underdal28, Susana Camargo Vieira29, Coleen Vogel30, Oran R Young31, Andrea Brock1 and Ruben Zondervan2 The current institutional framework for sustainable development is by far not strong enough to bring about the swift transformative progress that is needed. This article contends that incrementalism—the main approach since the 1972 Stockholm Conference—will not sufﬁce to bring about societal change at the level and speed needed to mitigate and adapt to earth system transformation. Instead, the article argues that transformative structural change in global governance is needed, and that the 2012 United Nations Conference on Sustainable Development in Rio de Janeiro must turn into a major stepping stone for a much stronger institutional framework for sustainable development. The article details core areas where urgent action is required. The article is based on an extensive social science assessment conducted by 32 members of the lead faculty, scientiﬁc steering committee, and other afﬁliates of the Earth System Governance Project. This Project is a ten-year research initiative under the auspices of the International Human Dimensions Programme on Global Environmental Change (IHDP), which is sponsored by the International Council for Science (ICSU), the International Social Science Council (ISSC), and the United Nations University (UNU). Addresses 1 VU University Amsterdam, Netherlands 2 Lund University, Sweden 3 Arizona State University, United States 4 Fridtjof Nansen Institute, Norway 5 University of Toronto, Canada 6 Colorado State University, United States 7 Durham University, United Kingdom 8 Yale University, United States 9 University of Waterloo, Canada 10 Stockholm **Resilience** Centre, Stockholm University, Sweden 11 Beijer Institute, Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, Sweden 12 Wageningen University and Research Centre, Netherlands 13 UNESCO-IHE Institute for Water Education, Delft, Netherlands 14 University of Massachusetts at Amherst, United States 15 Tyndall Centre, University of East Anglia, United Kingdom 16 Tokyo Institute of Technology, Japan 17 United Nations University Institute of Advanced Studies, Japan 18 CETIP, Slovak Academy of Sciences, Slovakia 19 Chiang Mai University, Thailand 20 University of Arizona, United States 21 Oxford University, United Kingdom 22 Carleton University, Canada 23 University of Oregon, United States 24 University of Sussex, United Kingdom 25 Vrije Universiteit Brussel, Belgium 26 El Colegio de la Frontera Norte, Tijuana, Mexico 27 University of California, Riverside, United States 28 University of Oslo, Norway 29 Universidade de Itau´ na, Brazil 30 Independent scholar, Johannesburg, South Africa 31 University of California at Santa Barbara, United States Corresponding author: Biermann, Frank (frank.biermann@vu.nl) Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability 2012, 4:51–60 This review comes from the Open issue Edited by Rik Leemans Received 16 September 2011; Accepted 27 January 2012 1877-3435/$ – see front matter Published by Elsevier B.V. DOI 10.1016/j.cosust.2012.01.014 Introduction Global environmental protection has been on the international political agenda since the 1972 UN Conference on the Human Environment. More than 900 environmental treaties are in force. Yet overall these efforts have not been effective in altering the decade-long trends of Available online at www.sciencedirect.com www.sciencedirect.com Current Opinion in Environmental Sustainability 2012, 4:51–60",2,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Substantiation/References,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of resilience; treat as noise unless context warrants.,78b9b04694,Transforming governance and institutions for global sustainability- key insights from the Earth System Governance Project,,10.1016/j.cosust.2012.01.014
