nuscript. Vienna convention for the protection of the ozone layer. 1985. International Legal Materials 26:1529-40. Weber, Steven. 1994. The European Bank for reconstruction and development. International Organization 48 (1): 1-38. Wendt, Alex. 1992. Anarchy is what states make of it: The social construction of power politics. International Organization 46:391-425. 1995. Constructing international politics. International Security 20:71-81. Williamson, Oliver. 1985. The economic institutions of capitalism. New York: Free Press. . 1994. The mechanisms of government. Oxford, UK: Oxford University Press. Yarbrough, Beth V., and Robert M. Yarbrough. 1992. Cooperation and governance in international trade. Princeton, NJ: Princeton University Press. Young, Oran R. 1994. International governance. Ithaca, NY: Cornell University Press. This content downloaded from 102.64.114.122 on Tue, 26 Nov 2024 20:22:45 UTC All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/terms",5,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Substantiation/References,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of conflict resolution; treat as noise unless context warrants.,565e8d44d1,Why states act through formal international organizations,,
stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z,stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z_0002334,False,organizational ecology and institutional-change-in-global governance.pdf,1,Unspecified,informal institutions,TH039,Principles Norms Rules,Pass 2 (theme + validated synonym dictionary),"Verbatim paragraph excerpt (PDF text layer), extracted at paragraph boundary","ert O. Keohane Abstract The institutions of global governance have changed dramatically in recent years. New organizational forms—including **informal institutions**, transgovernmental networks, and private tra ... nsnational regulatory organizations (PTROs)—have expanded rapidly, while the growth of formal intergovernmental organizations has slowed. Organizational ecology provides an insightful framework for understandin","Organizational Ecology and Institutional Change in Global Governance Kenneth W. Abbott, Jessica F. Green, and Robert O. Keohane Abstract The institutions of global governance have changed dramatically in recent years. New organizational forms—including **informal institutions**, transgovernmental networks, and private transnational regulatory organizations (PTROs)—have expanded rapidly, while the growth of formal intergovernmental organizations has slowed. Organizational ecology provides an insightful framework for understanding these changing patterns of growth. Organizational ecology is primarily a structural theory, emphasizing the influence of institutional environments, especially their organizational density and resource availability, on organizational behavior and viability. To demonstrate the explanatory value of organizational ecology, we analyze the proliferation of PTROs compared with the relative stasis of intergovernmental organizations (IGOs). Continued growth of IGOs is constrained by crowding in their dense institutional environment, but PTROs benefit from organizational flexibility and low entry costs, which allow them to enter “niches” with limited resource competition. We probe the plausibility of our analysis by examining contemporary climate governance. The institutions of global governance have changed dramatically in recent years. In the decades after 1945, dozens of specialized agencies, programs, and commissions were created within the UN system. Other functional intergovernmental organizations (IGOs), notably the World Trade Organization (WTO), appeared and gained influence, and European institutions expanded dramatically. Multilateral treaties also multiplied; environmental agreements, for example, grew by nearly 150 percent during the 1990s.1 In the early years of this century, however, growth in the number of IGOs has slowed markedly. Although existing IGOs continue to expand their activities, the formation of new organizations has decreased by about 20 percent.2 Adoption of new environmental treaties has slowed even more sharply.3 The authors are grateful for comments on earlier versions of this article received at the Princeton International Relations Colloquium on 5 November 2012, by the Political Science Faculty of the College of William and Mary on 15 April 2013, and at a conference on organizational ecology and world politics convened at Princeton, 6–8 March 2014. We are particularly grateful to Christina Davis, Tom Hale, Joseph S. Nye, Jon Pevehouse, Amy Quirk, Beth Simmons, and Maurits van der Veen and three anonymous reviewers for written comments on earlier versions of this article. 1. See Zelli and van Asselt 2013. 2. Pauwelyn, Wessel, and Wouters 2012. 3. Authors’ calculations based on data from Ronald B. Mitchell, 2002–12. International Environmental Agreements Database Project (version 2012.1), available at <http://iea.uoregon.edu/page.php?file=home. htm&query=static>, accessed 7 February 2015. International Organization 70, Spring 2016, pp. 247–277 © The IO Foundation, 2016 doi:10.1017/S0020818315000338 https://doi.org/10.1017/S0020818315000338 Published online by Cambridge University Press",5,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Explanation/Elaboration,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of principles norms rules; treat as noise unless context warrants.,8c68111741,organizational ecology and institutional-change-in-global governance,,10.1017/S0020818315000338
stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z,stage2_pass2full_20260125T233829Z_0002335,False,organizational ecology and institutional-change-in-global governance.pdf,2,Unspecified,fragmentation,TH001,Fragmentation,Pass 2 (theme + validated synonym dictionary),"Verbatim paragraph excerpt (PDF text layer), extracted at paragraph boundary","ave created informal institutions4 and plurilateral “clubs,”5 such as the G20 and other “G-groups.” In response to increasing institutional **fragmentation**, states have also established meta-institutions to ... coordinate other entities, such as the High Level Political Forum for sustainable development6 and the G20 for financial regulation.7 IGOs have created their own “emanations.”8 National regulatory agencies have","New organizational forms, in contrast, have emerged and expanded rapidly. States have created informal institutions4 and plurilateral “clubs,”5 such as the G20 and other “G-groups.” In response to increasing institutional **fragmentation**, states have also established meta-institutions to coordinate other entities, such as the High Level Political Forum for sustainable development6 and the G20 for financial regulation.7 IGOs have created their own “emanations.”8 National regulatory agencies have established influential transgovernmental institutions9 such as the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision.10 Subnational governments have also established transnational networks such as the C40 Cities Climate Leadership Group. Transnational public-private partnerships (PPPs) have expanded and gained official recognition, as at the 2002 World Summit on Sustainable Development.11 Perhaps most striking, private transnational regulatory organizations (PTROs) have proliferated. PTROs are established and governed by actors from civil society, business, and other sectors.12 They engage directly in transnational governance, adopting standards of conduct for business and other targets on regulatory issues from worker rights to climate change; promoting, monitoring, and enforcing those standards; and conducting related administrative activities.13 PTROs operate through markets, not through interstate negotiations or hierarchy;14 they adopt voluntary standards and rely on incentives such as consumer demand, reputational benefits, avoidance of mandatory regulation, and reduced transactions costs to induce participation and compliance.15 Before the 1990s, such organizations barely existed.16 Today, they are numerous, well established, and expanding in numbers.17 These changes are especially notable in climate change governance, on which we focus our empirical analysis. This dramatic shift in the institutional landscape is highly significant for global governance. Because PTROs are controlled by private actors rather than states, 4. Vabulas and Snidal 2013. 5. Keohane and Nye 2001. 6. Abbott and Bernstein 2015. 7. Viola 2015. 8. Shanks, Jacobson, and Kaplan 1996. 9. Keohane and Nye 1974. 10. Slaughter 2004. 11. See Keohane and Nye 1974; Slaughter 2004; Betsill and Bulkeley 2006; Andonova 2010; and Bäckstrand 2008. 12. See Abbott and Snidal 2009a and 2009b. On the public-private distinction, see Pattberg and Stripple 2008. 13. Such activities include operating accreditation systems for monitors and registries of certified projects. Private transnational organizations, a more inclusive class, also perform other functions, such as managing operational projects and disseminating information. Abbott 2012. We focus here on the subset of regulatory organizations. 14. Lake 2009. 15. See Keohane 1984; Cashore, Auld, and Newsom 2004; Prakash and Potoski 2006; and Buthe 2010. 16. See Abbott and Snidal 2009b; and Hale and Roger 2014. 17. Robert Axelrod has suggested to us that the Internet, which did not exist before 1990, may have facilitated the growth of PTROs by facilitating their ability to create a transnational brand (Axelrod, personal communication, 30 September 2015). 248 International Organization https://doi.org/10.1017/S0020818315000338 Published online by Cambridge University Press",9,True,SUGGEST_REJECT,PENDING,,Full corpus Pass 2 (synonym-expanded) — remaining documents,Explanation/Elaboration,Draft memo: appears to be bibliographic/administrative text rather than substantive discussion of fragmentation; treat as noise unless context warrants.,8c68111741,organizational ecology and institutional-change-in-global governance,,10.1017/S0020818315000338
