ing, monitoring, and enforcing those standards; and conducting related administrative activities.13 PTROs operate through markets, not through interstate negotiations or hierarchy;14 they adopt voluntary standards and rely on incentives such as consumer demand, reputational benefits, avoidance of mandatory regulation, and reduced transactions costs to induce participation and **compliance**.15 Before the 1990s, such organizations barely existed.16 Today, they are numerous, well established, and expanding in numbers.17 These changes are especially notable in climate change governance, on which we focus our empirical analysis. This dramatic shift in the institutional landscape is highly significant for global governance. Because PTROs are controlled by private actors rather than states, 4. Vabulas and Snidal 2013. 5. Keohane and Nye 2001. 6. Abbott and Bernstein 2015. 7. Viola 2015. 8. Shanks, Jacobson, and Kaplan 1996. 9. Keohane and Nye 1974. 10. Slaughter 2004. 11. See Keohane and Nye 1974; Slaughter 2004; Betsill and Bulkeley 2006; Andonova 2010; and Bäckstrand 2008. 12. See Abbott and Snidal 2009a and 2009b. On the public-private distinction, see Pattberg and Stripple 2008. 13. Such activities include operating accreditation systems for monitors and registries of certified projects. Private transnational organizations, a more inclusive class, also perform other functions, such as managing operational projects and disseminating information. Abbott 2012. We focus here on the subset of regulatory organizations. 14. Lake 2009. 15. See Keohane 1984; Cashore, Auld, and Newsom 2004; Prakash and Potoski 2006; and Buthe 2010. 16. See Abbott and Snidal 2009b; and Hale and Roger 2014. 17. Robert Axelrod has suggested to us that the Internet, which did not exist before 1990, may have facilitated the growth of PTROs by facilitating their ability to create a transnational brand (Axelrod, personal communication, 30 September 2015). 248 International Organization https://doi.org